Context (pp. 71–73): Grothendieck is axiomatising 'tame topology' (topologie modérée, as announced in Esquisse d'un programme) in combinatorial terms. The basic objects are a set M of 'strata' or 'figures' with relations: ≪ (refinement), ≤ (subdivision), compatibility, and ∥ (disjointness). Page 71 handles the one-dimensional case: an ordered set I, finite subsets Δ, and the Boolean algebra generated by the 'points' {a_i} and the open intervals ]a_i,a_{i+1}[.

This page, step by step:
(1) A figure F has a finite set F̃ of elementary pieces (its strata). The tame parts adapted to F are exactly the unions of such pieces, so they identify with the power set 𝔓(F̃).
(2) Suppose F' is a subdivision or refinement of F (F' ≪ F). Each fine piece lies in a unique coarse piece. This gives a canonical surjection φ: F̃' → F̃. Its inverse image φ*: 𝔓(F̃) → 𝔓(F̃') is an injective homomorphism of Boolean algebras.
(3) The system (𝔓(F̃), φ*) is directed only along refinement. Figures that are neither compatible, nor related by ≪, nor 'pre-compatible' (compatible after subdividing both) are not linked directly. So Grothendieck does not want to assume a priori that all the 𝔓(F̃) can be read as parts of one common set 𝔓(P), with P a set of 'points' attached to the structure.
(4) Instead he announces an axiomatic notion: a 'point realisation' of the structure in a set P of points. Page 73 makes this precise as a map M → 𝔓(P), X ↦ |X|, satisfying:
- monotonicity for ≪;
- intersections of non-disjoint strata are unions of the |Z| for Z in X∩Y;
- X ∥ Y if and only if |X| ∩ |Y| = ∅;
- X ≼ Y implies |X| = |Y|;
- additivity over the combinatorial decomposition comb(X);
- covering and T0-separation (with the T0 quotient by M_x as a normalisation).
This is in essence a Stone-type representation problem. One wants a set of points for the colimit of the finite Boolean algebras 𝔓(F̃). The canonical candidate is the inverse limit of the finite sets F̃ (an ultrafilter or Stone space), but only once compatibility is available.
